## formula
formula-electrical-resistance-temperature = How a metal’s resistance depends on temperature
formula-electrical-resistance-temperature-r-name = resistance of the conductor when warmed
formula-electrical-resistance-temperature-r-name-2 = resistance at the starting temperature
formula-electrical-resistance-temperature-variables-name = temperature coefficient of resistance
formula-electrical-resistance-temperature-meaning-school = by what share the resistance grows when the conductor warms by 1 °C; for copper ≈ 0.0039
formula-electrical-resistance-temperature-t-name = how much the conductor warmed
formula-electrical-resistance-temperature-explanation-novice = The hotter a metal, the more it “gets in the way” of a current: its resistance grows.
formula-electrical-resistance-temperature-explanation-school = The resistance of a metal conductor grows with temperature: R = R₀·(1 + α·Δt). Hence the temperature coefficient α = (R − R₀) / (R₀·Δt) — by what share the resistance grows per degree.
formula-electrical-cell-resistance = Resistance of the liquid between the electrodes
formula-electrical-cell-resistance-r-name = resistance
formula-electrical-cell-resistance-k-name = cell constant
formula-electrical-cell-resistance-meaning-school = the gap L between the electrodes divided by their area S: the farther apart and the smaller the plates, the larger the resistance
formula-electrical-cell-resistance-variables-name = electrical conductivity of the liquid
formula-electrical-cell-resistance-meaning-school-2 = how well the liquid conducts; depends on how many ions it holds and on the temperature
formula-electrical-cell-resistance-explanation-novice = Pure water hardly lets current through, salt water does it well: dissolved salt gives charged particles that carry the current.
formula-electrical-cell-resistance-explanation-school = It is the same conductor formula R = ρ·L/S, only for a liquid: ρ = 1/κ. The longer the path and the smaller the electrodes, the larger the resistance.
formula-electrical-cell-resistance-explanation-expert = Parallel-plate model without fringe effects; κ from a table by composition with a linear temperature correction.
formula-electrical-cell-current = Current through the liquid (Ohm’s law)
formula-electrical-cell-current-i-name = current
formula-electrical-cell-current-u-name = source voltage
formula-electrical-cell-current-u-name-2 = decomposition voltage
formula-electrical-cell-current-meaning-school = the least voltage at which the current starts to split the liquid; below it there is almost no current
formula-electrical-cell-current-r-name = resistance of the liquid
formula-electrical-cell-current-r-name-2 = internal resistance of the source
formula-electrical-cell-current-explanation-novice = The higher the voltage and the lower the resistance, the larger the current.
formula-electrical-cell-current-explanation-school = Ohm’s law: I = U/R. In a liquid part of the voltage goes into splitting it at the electrodes (U_d) and part is “lost” inside the battery (r), so the current is counted from what is left.
formula-electrical-cell-current-explanation-expert = U_d is the thermodynamic minimum from standard potentials; electrode overpotentials are ignored, so the model somewhat overestimates the current at low voltages.
formula-electrical-joule-power = Heating power of a current (Joule–Lenz law)
formula-electrical-joule-power-p-name = heating power
formula-electrical-joule-power-explanation-novice = A current heats whatever it passes through. Twice the current heats four times as much.
formula-electrical-joule-power-explanation-school = The Joule–Lenz law: in time t the heat Q = I²·R·t is released. That is how immersion heaters and electric hobs work.
formula-electrical-faraday-moles = How much gas a current releases (Faraday’s law)
formula-electrical-faraday-moles-n-name = amount of gas
formula-electrical-faraday-moles-q-name = charge passed through the liquid
formula-electrical-faraday-moles-z-name = electrons per molecule
formula-electrical-faraday-moles-meaning-school = electrons needed per gas molecule: 2 for hydrogen and chlorine, 4 for oxygen
formula-electrical-faraday-moles-f-name = Faraday constant
formula-electrical-faraday-moles-meaning-school-2 = 96 485 C — the charge of one mole of electrons
formula-electrical-faraday-moles-explanation-novice = The more charge passes through the liquid, the more gas forms at the electrodes.
formula-electrical-faraday-moles-explanation-school = Faraday’s law: the mass released at an electrode is proportional to the charge q = I·t. One mole of hydrogen needs 2 moles of electrons, that is 2 × 96 485 C.
formula-electrical-ohm-resistance = Resistance of a conductor (Ohm’s law for part of a circuit)
formula-electrical-ohm-resistance-r-name = resistance of the conductor
formula-electrical-ohm-resistance-u-name = voltage across the conductor
formula-electrical-ohm-resistance-meaning-school = shown by a voltmeter connected in parallel with the conductor
formula-electrical-ohm-resistance-i-name = current in the conductor
formula-electrical-ohm-resistance-meaning-school-2 = shown by an ammeter connected in series
formula-electrical-ohm-resistance-explanation-novice = Divide the voltage by the current — and you know how much the conductor “holds back” the current.
formula-electrical-ohm-resistance-explanation-school = Ohm’s law for part of a circuit: I = U / R, so R = U / I. For a metal conductor at a steady temperature the resistance does not depend on the voltage: twice the voltage, twice the current.
formula-electrical-series-voltage = Voltage in series
formula-electrical-series-voltage-u-name = total voltage
formula-electrical-series-voltage-u-name-2 = voltage across the first conductor
formula-electrical-series-voltage-u-name-3 = voltage across the second conductor
formula-electrical-series-voltage-explanation-novice = The source’s voltage is shared by conductors one after another.
formula-electrical-series-voltage-explanation-school = The current in a series circuit is the same, so U₁ = I·R₁, U₂ = I·R₂: more voltage across the larger resistance, and together the whole: U = U₁ + U₂.
formula-electrical-parallel-current = Current in parallel
formula-electrical-parallel-current-i-name = current in the main line
formula-electrical-parallel-current-i-name-2 = current in the first branch
formula-electrical-parallel-current-i-name-3 = current in the second branch
formula-electrical-parallel-current-explanation-novice = The current splits like water at a fork of pipes, and meets again.
formula-electrical-parallel-current-explanation-school = The voltage across the branches is the same, so I₁ = U/R₁, I₂ = U/R₂: more current in the branch of smaller resistance, and together the whole: I = I₁ + I₂.
formula-electrical-power = Power of a consumer
formula-electrical-power-p-name = power
formula-electrical-power-u-name = voltage across the consumer
formula-electrical-power-i-name = current in the consumer
formula-electrical-power-explanation-novice = Multiply the voltmeter by the ammeter — and you know how many watts the device takes.
formula-electrical-power-explanation-school = P = U·I. For a resistor I = U/R, so P = U²/R: twice the voltage — four times the power.
formula-electrical-compass-field = A field from a compass’s turn
formula-electrical-compass-field-b-name = the electromagnet’s field at the compass
formula-electrical-compass-field-b-name-2 = the horizontal part of the Earth’s field
formula-electrical-compass-field-variables-name = the angle the needle turned
formula-electrical-compass-field-explanation-novice = The Earth pulls the needle north, the electromagnet sideways. The stronger the electromagnet, the farther the needle turns.
formula-electrical-compass-field-explanation-school = The electromagnet’s field B is across the Earth’s horizontal field B_E; the needle lines up with their sum, so tg θ = B / B_E. The Earth’s field in Kyiv ≈ 19 µT (the WMM-2025 model).
formula-electrical-induced-emf = The induced EMF
formula-electrical-induced-emf-variables-name = the induced EMF
formula-electrical-induced-emf-i-name = the induced current
formula-electrical-induced-emf-r-name = the circuit’s resistance: the coil’s and the milliammeter’s
formula-electrical-induced-emf-explanation-novice = The faster you move the magnet, the stronger the current in the coil.
formula-electrical-induced-emf-explanation-school = The induced EMF is proportional to how fast the flux changes and to the number of turns: ε = −N·ΔΦ/Δt. The current it drives round the circuit is I = ε / R — so from the milliammeter the EMF is ε = I·R.
